text-100">NAB Senior Frontend Engineer Technical Interview</h1>
<h2 class=3D"text-xl font-bold text-text-100 mt-1 -mb-0.5">Part 1: Warm-up =
&amp; Introduction</h2>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Interviewer</strong>: Go=
od morning! I'm Sarah, Principal Frontend Engineer here at NAB. I've been w=
ith the bank for about 8 years now, leading our digital banking platform de=
velopment. Thanks for taking the time to interview with us today. Could you=
 start by telling me a bit about yourself and your journey in frontend deve=
lopment?</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Candidate</strong>: Good=
 morning Sarah, thank you for having me. I'm excited to be here. I've been =
working in frontend development for about 7 years now, with the last 4 year=
s focused heavily on React ecosystem and state management solutions. I star=
ted my career at a fintech startup where I learned the importance of scalab=
le architecture and performance optimization - something I imagine is cruci=
al here at NAB given the scale of your digital banking operations.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">Most recently, I've been working=
 as a Senior Frontend Engineer at a large e-commerce platform where I led t=
he migration from a legacy jQuery-based system to a modern React/TypeScript=
 stack. We handled about 2 million daily active users, which taught me a lo=
t about state management at scale, particularly with Redux and more modern =
alternatives like Zustand and Jotai.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Interviewer</strong>: Th=
at's impressive experience, especially the migration work. At NAB, we're co=
nstantly evolving our frontend architecture. Speaking of state management, =
I see from your resume you have extensive experience with Redux. Can you wa=
lk me through your understanding of Redux and particularly why reducers are=
 so fundamental to the pattern?</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Candidate</strong>: Abso=
lutely! Redux has been a cornerstone of my development approach, especially=
 in large-scale applications. I like to think of Redux using what I call th=
e "bank analogy" - which seems fitting for our conversation today.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Redux is essentially lik=
e a well-organized bank with three core departments:</strong></p>
<ol class=3D"[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:p=
b-1 list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>The Store (Bank Vault)<=
/strong> - This is our single source of truth, the central repository where=
 all application state lives</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Actions (Transaction Re=
quests)</strong> - These are the standardized forms customers fill out to r=
equest changes</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Reducers (Bank Officers=
)</strong> - These are the rule-based processors that determine how to hand=
le each transaction</li>
</ol>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Reducers are fundamental=
 because they're the only mechanism through which state can be modified.</s=
trong> They enforce three critical principles that I've seen prevent countl=
ess bugs in production:</p>
