<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>The beauty of reducers i=
s their predictability.</strong> In my last project, we had a complex finan=
cial dashboard where users could apply multiple filters, sort options, and =
data transformations. By keeping all these operations in pure reducers, we =
could:</p>
<ul class=3D"[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:p=
b-1 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Time-travel debug</stro=
ng> by replaying actions</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Unit test easily</stron=
g> by passing in state + action and asserting the output</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Implement undo/redo fun=
ctionality</strong> by maintaining action history</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Hot-reload development<=
/strong> without losing application state</li>
</ul>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Interviewer</strong>: Ex=
cellent explanation! I really like the bank analogy. Now, let's dive deeper=
. In your experience with large-scale applications, how do you handle compl=
ex state updates that might involve multiple nested objects? And what are y=
our thoughts on the immutability requirements?</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Candidate</strong>: Grea=
t question! <strong>Nested state updates are where Redux can become either =
elegant or a nightmare, depending on how you approach it.</strong> In my ex=
perience, there are several strategies I've evolved over the years:</p>
<h2 class=3D"text-xl font-bold text-text-100 mt-1 -mb-0.5">Strategy 1: Norm=
alize Your State Structure</h2>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">First, I always advocate for <st=
rong>state normalization</strong>, especially for complex data. Instead of =
deeply nested objects, I structure state like a database:</p>
