<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>The key insight I've gai=
ned is that immutability isn't just about preventing bugs - it enables powe=
rful features like:</strong></p>
<ul class=3D"[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:p=
b-1 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Optimistic updates</str=
ong> with easy rollback</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Time-travel debugging</=
strong> for complex user interactions</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Memoization and perform=
ance optimization</strong> with selectors</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>State persistence and h=
ydration</strong> for offline-first applications</li>
</ul>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Interviewer</strong>: Th=
at's a very thorough answer! I'm particularly impressed with your normaliza=
tion approach and the real-world financial example. At NAB, we deal with si=
milar complexity in our transaction processing systems.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">Now, let's talk about performanc=
e. You mentioned memoization and selectors. In a high-traffic application l=
ike our digital banking platform, how do you optimize Redux performance, es=
pecially when dealing with large datasets and frequent state updates?</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Candidate</strong>: Perf=
ormance optimization in Redux for high-traffic applications is something I'=
m passionate about, especially having worked with applications handling mil=
lions of transactions. <strong>At NAB's scale, every millisecond matters</s=
trong> for user experience and customer satisfaction.</p>
<h2 class=3D"text-xl font-bold text-text-100 mt-1 -mb-0.5">1. Selector Opti=
mization with Reselect</h2>
