<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>The future is definitely=
 moving toward more specialized, composable solutions rather than monolithi=
c state management.</strong> The key is choosing the right tool for each sp=
ecific problem rather than forcing everything into one pattern.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Interviewer</strong>: Fa=
scinating insights! Your hybrid approach and the distinction between server=
 and client state really resonates with our current architectural discussio=
ns at NAB. I can see you've thought deeply about this evolution.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">Now, let's move into some more p=
ractical territory. Let's say you're tasked with implementing a real-time t=
ransaction monitoring dashboard for our fraud detection team. This dashboar=
d needs to display thousands of transactions per minute, allow real-time fi=
ltering, and provide instant alerts. How would you architect the frontend s=
tate management for this requirement?</p>
<h2 class=3D"text-xl font-bold text-text-100 mt-1 -mb-0.5">Part 2: Deep Tec=
hnical Discussion</h2>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Candidate</strong>: This=
 is exactly the kind of challenge I love! <strong>Real-time fraud detection=
 is one of the most demanding frontend scenarios</strong> - you're dealing =
with high-frequency data, low-latency requirements, and critical user workf=
lows where every millisecond matters for potentially stopping fraudulent tr=
ansactions.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">Let me walk you through my archi=
tectural approach:</p>
<h2 class=3D"text-xl font-bold text-text-100 mt-1 -mb-0.5">1. Multi-layered=
 State Architecture</h2>
