<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Key Memory Management St=
rategies:</strong></p>
<ol class=3D"[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:p=
b-1 list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Proactive Index Cleanup=
</strong>: Clear and rebuild indices rather than letting them grow indefini=
tely</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Memory Leak Detection</=
strong>: Track heap growth patterns and index size changes</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Automatic Cleanup Trigg=
ers</strong>: Force cleanup when memory usage exceeds thresholds</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Selector Cache Manageme=
nt</strong>: Clear reselect caches when memory pressure is high</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Early Termination</stro=
ng>: Limit result sets to prevent excessive memory allocation</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Garbage Collection Hint=
s</strong>: Trigger GC when available in development environments</li>
</ol>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">This approach ensures that the f=
iltering system can run reliably in production environments for extended pe=
riods without memory issues.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Interviewer</strong>: Ou=
tstanding! Your memory management approach is exactly what we need for prod=
uction systems. The proactive cleanup and automatic leak detection are part=
icularly valuable.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Follow-up 2</strong>: Lo=
oking at your indexing strategy, what happens when we have real-time update=
s coming in at high frequency? How do you balance keeping the indices fresh=
 with performance, especially when new transactions might change the optima=
l filtering strategy?</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Candidate</strong>: Fant=
astic question! <strong>Real-time index management is one of the most chall=
enging aspects of high-frequency financial systems.</strong> The key is und=
erstanding that with streaming data, you can't afford full index rebuilds o=
n every update, but you also can't let indices become stale.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">Let me show you an <strong>incre=
mental index management system</strong> that I've developed for exactly thi=
s scenario:</p>
